   291	
   292	- **Directions per parameter (audit table).** Weaker claim when:
   293	  θ ↑, θrec ↑, η ↑, arming lags A and A_f ↑, ρ ↑, recoveryFull false → true,
   294	  proposer gap G ↓, outage budget Π ↓, faultCap ↓, persistent-inclusion
   295	  delay D ↑. There is no rule of the form "premise parameters go one
   296	  way": each parameter declares its direction, and a monotonicity lemma
   297	  proves it.
   298	- **Exact-time inclusion is not monotone in D (audit B02).**
   299	  `IncludedFrom … D` requires containment exactly at `s + D`; an output
   300	  can hold B at `s + D` and drop it before `s + D′`. So:
   301	  - `ac.included.v1` (exact time) stays, for the `ac-4.3` equivalence; it
   302	    has no D-monotonicity lemma;
   303	  - `ac.includedPersist.v1`: B is in every honest read of `o` at every
   304	    time from `s + D` to the horizon. It is monotone in D;
   305	  - relation lemma: `ac.included.v1 ∧ ac.monotone.v1` (same instance, same
   306	    output) implies `ac.includedPersist.v1`. The same applies to stable,
   307	    fast, recovered and strong inclusion.
   308	- **Conditional relation lemmas.** A relation lemma may use other
   309	  certificates of the same instance as premises. This is possible only
   310	  because all certificates share one interface (§3).
   311	- **Regimes with a minimum horizon (audit M05).** Outage, FastOutage,
   312	  Recovery and StrongArmed require a minimum horizon (b₁, GST + ρ, b₀).
   313	  They are closed under time truncation only above that bound (audit
   314	  table). The composition (§7) uses causal step lemmas with explicit
   315	  horizon bounds, not "prefix-closed + induction on time".
