   230	## 3. Instances and facets (L2, L4)
   231	
   232	**One interface per instance (audit B01).** An instance is one record:
   233	
   234	```lean
   235	structure Instance where
   236	  P       : ProtocolSpec
   237	  facets  : FacetBundle P     -- every facet the instance exposes, once
   238	  params  : ParamBinding      -- one value per named parameter
   239	  hooks   : HooksSound P
   240	  iso     : RecordIsolated P
   241	```
   242	
   243	Every certificate of the instance uses this same `facets` and `params`.
   244	A claim reads the facets and the named parameters that it needs by
   245	projection. Two claims that use the output `stable`, or the parameter
   246	`η`, therefore see the same getter and the same value. This rules out
   247	the v4 attack in which one certificate used a genesis getter for safety
   248	and another certificate used a different getter for inclusion.
   249	
   250	Facets (append-only):
   251	
   252	```text
   253	┌──────────────┬──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   254	│ Facet        │ Content                                              │
   255	├──────────────┼──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   256	│ ChainOutputs │ named outputs `o : State → Block`                    │
   257	│ Proposals    │ proposal labels, label times, proposal identifier,   │
   258	│              │ `block : Payload → Option Block`, `opening`          │
   259	│ Protected    │ `protected : State → Block → Bool` (for SA: live     │
   260	│              │ leases ∪ S)                                          │
   261	│ FGTarget     │ the AP's target at the FG action (§7)                │
   262	│ FinalityHead │ the AP head at the FG action (§7)                    │
   263	│ AnchorChoice │ the AP's rule to pick one anchor (§7)                │
   264	│ Base         │ the record-isolation projection (E5)                 │
   265	│ Durable      │ `durable`, `restore` (E6)                            │
   266	└──────────────┴──────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   267	```
   268	
   269	Every output getter reads only `base`. Claims are schemas over output
   270	names (`Safe o`, `Monotone o`, `Included o`, …). A single-ledger family
   271	maps `stable` and `confirmed` to one getter.
   272	
   273	Protocol-defined internal facets (`Protected`, `FGTarget`) are sound for
   274	composition: both sides of a composition theorem use the same facet of
   275	the same instance.
   276	
   277	## 4. Claim catalog (L3)
   278	
   279	```lean
   280	structure ClaimSpec where
   281	  id         : Lean.Name                     -- immutable, e.g. `ac.safe.v1`
   282	  needs      : FacetNeeds                    -- which facets it reads
   283	  params     : List ParamName                -- which named parameters
   284	  dir        : ParamName → Direction         -- per parameter: `up` or
   285	                                             -- `down` makes the claim weaker
   286	  valid      : ParamBinding → Prop
   287	  holds      : (I : Instance) → Prop         -- over I.facets, I.params
   288	  inhabited  : (I : Instance) → Prop         -- RegimeInhabited (§5)
   289	  refutable  : RefutationKind                -- finite run, or family
   290	```
   291	
   292	- **Directions per parameter (audit table).** Weaker claim when:
   293	  θ ↑, θrec ↑, η ↑, arming lags A and A_f ↑, ρ ↑, recoveryFull false → true,
   294	  proposer gap G ↓, outage budget Π ↓, faultCap ↓, persistent-inclusion
   295	  delay D ↑. There is no rule of the form "premise parameters go one
   296	  way": each parameter declares its direction, and a monotonicity lemma
   297	  proves it.
   298	- **Exact-time inclusion is not monotone in D (audit B02).**
   299	  `IncludedFrom … D` requires containment exactly at `s + D`; an output
   300	  can hold B at `s + D` and drop it before `s + D′`. So:
   301	  - `ac.included.v1` (exact time) stays, for the `ac-4.3` equivalence; it
   302	    has no D-monotonicity lemma;
   303	  - `ac.includedPersist.v1`: B is in every honest read of `o` at every
   304	    time from `s + D` to the horizon. It is monotone in D;
   305	  - relation lemma: `ac.included.v1 ∧ ac.monotone.v1` (same instance, same
   306	    output) implies `ac.includedPersist.v1`. The same applies to stable,
   307	    fast, recovered and strong inclusion.
   308	- **Conditional relation lemmas.** A relation lemma may use other
   309	  certificates of the same instance as premises. This is possible only
   310	  because all certificates share one interface (§3).
   311	- **Regimes with a minimum horizon (audit M05).** Outage, FastOutage,
   312	  Recovery and StrongArmed require a minimum horizon (b₁, GST + ρ, b₀).
   313	  They are closed under time truncation only above that bound (audit
   314	  table). The composition (§7) uses causal step lemmas with explicit
   315	  horizon bounds, not "prefix-closed + induction on time".
   316	- **Validity of parameters.** `valid` is checked for every certificate
   317	  and every refutation.
   318	
   319	**Profiles.** A profile is a named set of catalog ids plus the parameter
   320	names it binds. A profile certificate is a set of certificates of ONE
   321	instance that covers the profile. `ac-4.3` is the current bundle, with a
   322	proved equivalence to `AvailableChain`; `strong-1` is `StrongClaims`.
   323	`RecoveredAt` is split into per-claim recovered entries (async Q5).
   324	
   325	Tiers:
   326	
   327	```text
   328	┌──────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
   329	│ Tier         │ Entries                                               │
   330	├──────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
   331	│ core         │ safe(confirmed), safe(stable), prefix(stable ⪯        │
   332	│              │ confirmed), includedPersist(confirmed)                │
   333	│ standard     │ includedPersist(stable) under StableSleepy, fast      │
   334	│              │ inclusion, persistence (general and fast), recovered  │
   335	│              │ variants                                              │
   336	│ extra        │ strong claims; `base.coherent`, `base.sourceTransfer`;│
   337	│              │ P1 from a recovered handoff; `noFutureRead`; anything │
   338	│              │ added later                                           │
   339	│ fg-ready     │ AP-side FG entries (§7)                               │
   340	│ composite    │ derived by `Kit.Compose` (§7)                         │
   341	└──────────────┴───────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
   342	```
   343	
   344	`monotone` is not a separate core entry: `Safe` includes monotonicity.
   345	
   346	## 5. Certificates, refutations, registry (L4, L6)
   347	
   348	```lean
   349	structure Cert (c : ClaimSpec) (I : Instance) where
   350	  valid     : c.valid I.params
   351	  inhabited : c.inhabited I
   352	  proof     : c.holds I
   353	
   354	structure Refutation (c : ClaimSpec) (I : Instance) where
   355	  valid     : c.valid I.params
   356	  proof     : ¬ c.holds I
   357	```
   358	
   359	**Non-vacuity (audit B03, M06).** One short witness run is not enough:
   360	a protocol can deliver a heartbeat in a short run while a longer run
   361	cannot meet the broadcast deadline of an undeliverable object. So
   362	`inhabited` requires arbitrary-horizon inhabitation: for every horizon H
   363	there is a run of THIS instance in the claim's regime with horizon ≥ H,
   364	an honest emission delivered, and a fault and a sleeper where the regime
   365	allows them. Boundary cases are allowed: no fault when the regime forbids
   366	one (faultCap = 0; Fast at n = 4). `Kit.Engine` proves inhabitation once
   367	for the canonical synchronous scheduler from honest deliverability (E4).
   368	Premise strengthening (`dir`) transports `holds`, not `inhabited`;
   369	inhabitation is re-proved or transported separately.
   370	
   371	**Refutations (audit M08).**
   372	- Every catalog claim has a deadline and a finite horizon. A bounded
   373	  claim is refuted by one finite run; "no parameter value works" is
   374	  refuted by a parameterized family of finite runs, as the existing
   375	  Sanity theorem does for every D. Lasso refutations need infinite-run
   376	  semantics and are out of scope (§0).
   377	- Kernel checking: `native_decide` is not allowed. Refutations use
   378	  `decide_cbv` (available in the pinned toolchain) or a reflective
   379	  checker with a soundness theorem. The checker must establish the
   380	  execution, the regime premises and the failed conclusion, not only
   381	  simulate the protocol. A scaled trace refutes the original instance only
   382	  with a proved scaling correspondence. Executable checkers exist only
   383	  for instances that provide an executable interface with equivalence
   384	  proofs; the framework does not promise a checker for every entry.
   385	
   386	**Registry (audit M06).**
   387	- Every parameter type has a decidable comparison with a soundness proof
   388	  against its `dir`. The registry reports, per instance and claim, the
   389	  Pareto set of proved parameter bindings and the refuted bindings, or
   390	  open. It does not assume one strongest tuple.
   391	- `@[cert]` and `@[refutes]` attributes are registered by code that
   392	  checks the theorem's type against `Cert c I` or `Refutation c I`.
   393	
   394	**Freeze (audit M09).**
   395	- `catalog.lock` holds, for each L1, LM, L2 and L3 declaration, a hash of
   396	  the transitive closure of its dependencies: declaration types and
   397	  bodies, inductive types, and opaque bodies
   398	  (`ConstantInfo.getUsedConstantsAsSet`).
   399	- Each L0 and LM version has its own frozen content hash. An in-place edit
   400	  of a released version fails CI.
   401	- The lock also pins the toolchain and the Lake manifest.
   402	- Each certificate records its instance, facets, parameter binding and
   403	  dependency hashes.
   404	- The freeze check detects changed meaning. It does not replace kernel
   405	  checking.
   406	
   407	## 6. Kits and explorer (L5, L6)
   408	
   409	- `Kit.Engine`: honest origin of signed objects; transfer within Δ after
   410	  GST and on a healthy prefix; relay closure; clean start; the canonical
   411	  scheduler and arbitrary-horizon inhabitation (§5). Proved once.
   412	- `Kit.Goldfish`: the shared substrate of Grid CO, SA, SA-PC and L*.
   413	  Optional.
   414	- `Kit.FG`: proofs about the fixed Simplex FG (LM definitions).
   415	- `Kit.Compose`: the composition theorems (§7).
   416	- Explorer: executable instances; checkers for regimes and conclusions on
   417	  finite runs; found runs are emitted as Lean refutation files.
   418	
   419	## 7. FG composition
   420	
   421	_Round 5 (async session) supplied the forms below. The exact final
   422	statements of (A) and (B) come from r147 and blueprint-opus; they enter
   423	the catalog as versioned entries and do not change this structure._
   424	
   425	### 7.1 Decisions (user, 2026-09-28)
   426	
   427	- The FG is fixed: our Simplex FG (the DC §7 finality gadget, without SG,
   428	  grades or safe votes). Its definitions are in LM; its lemmas are proved
   429	  once in `Kit.FG`.
   430	- APs vary. An AP plugs in by proving the AP-side entries.
   431	- FG heights never skip.
   432	- Every height pair (target or timeout) needs the AP's certified source;
   433	  with no source the pair is empty (certified pairs).
   434	- **Interference is relative to a height.** If height h* ends up
   435	  finalized (even before this is known), then from h* + 1 there is no FG
   436	  interference: the root can still change (the J at h* is revealed), but
   437	  every valid block of height > h* extends F*, so the change only prunes
   438	  branches that never reached h* + 1. The NJ arm (m = H + 1) and the
   439	  finalized arm (m = h* + 1) are two cases of "no interference from
   440	  height m". A fixed filter is only a special case.
   441	- Fallback: an AP that cannot meet the AP-side entries is certified
   442	  white-box as one composite instance.
   443	
   444	### 7.2 Interface
   445	
   446	**Anchor function (LM).** `anchorsOf : View → Block × Finset Block`
   447	returns `(F, anchors)` for a duty view V_d.
   448	- `View` is defined formally: the processed blocks of the duty's frozen
   449	  view (the frozen HEAD view, the full proposal view), closed under the
   450	  F evidence basis (the accepting carrier, the J carrier, a descendant
   451	  witness; r143 §2.1). The basis stays in every duty view across the
   452	  F-advance cut, so F stays viable in frozen views.
   453	- It is evaluated at duty time on the duty's own view (R1). R1 is a
   454	  DELIBERATE DEVIATION from DC, which reads live control caches (audit
   455	  M04; fgsa-fast.md). The correspondence theorem is proved for the
   456	  duty-view version.
   457	- `anchors` = the viable entries at FG height h_max − 1 above the root;
   458	  {J} when the root is J (h_max = h_j + 1); {genesis} while h_max = 1.
   459	- Correspondence theorem (`Kit.FG`, versioned): DC's viable set in V_d
   460	  equals the blocks on root–anchor paths plus anchor descendants in V_d,
   461	  under the entry invariants, the admission rule, the F-advance cut and
   462	  frozen branches. Two orders stay apart: in the chain transition,
   463	  finality comes before height; the store update changes h_max, then J,
   464	  then F.
   465	- `process` does only FG admission (F ⪯ B) for the choice tree.
   466	
   467	**AP facets.** `FGTarget` (the height-pair source body), `FinalityHead`
   468	(the DC finality pair comes from the AP head at the action, not from
   469	`FGTarget`), `AnchorChoice` (only the HEAD and proposal walks read the
   470	anchors; other AP rules may use the raw tree), and row inclusion.

