   850	\section{Finality gadget in the fork-choice}\label{sec:fg-fork-choice}
   851	
   852	\subsection{Stored states and finality updates}
   853	
   854	The store adds a block state map $\Sigma.\sigma[\cdot]$ and finality state
   855	$(\Sigma.F,\Sigma.J,\Sigma.h_j,\Sigma.\hmax)$.
   856	Figure~\ref{alg:fg-store} extends the block handler: it admits only
   857	descendants of the finalized block, computes and stores
   858	the post-state, then folds it into the finality caches with
   859	$\Call{update\_finality}{\Sigma,\sigma}$.  The pair
   860	$(\Sigma.J,\Sigma.h_j)$ tracks the lex-greatest justification event
   861	compatible with the finalized block.  $\Sigma.F$ advances
   862	only to a viable proper descendant of itself below~$\Sigma.J$, so
   863	$\Sigma.F\preceq\Sigma.J$ always holds and finalization never reverts.
   864	$\Sigma.\hmax$ only grows, and every block that raises it descends the
   865	finalized block at that moment.  When $\Sigma.F$ advances, an admitted
   866	block can stop descending it and its branch freezes: its children now
   867	fail admission.  The viability guard on the advance then keeps
   868	$\Sigma.\hmax$ at most one above the largest state height among the
   869	finalized block's descendants, so $\Sigma.F$ itself is always viable.
   870	The figure follows the viability definitions it uses.
   871	
   872	\subsection{Fork choice}
   873	
   874	\begin{definition}[Viability]
   875	\label{def:fg-candidate-tree}
   876	A descendant of the finalized block is viable when one of its
   877	descendants has state height at most one below the current maximum.
   878	Figure~\ref{alg:fg-store} defines
   879	$\Call{viable\_tree\_from}{\Sigma,\mathit{blocks}}$ over an explicit
   880	processed-block view.  The ordinary
   881	$\Call{viable\_tree}{\Sigma}$ uses $\Sigma.\T$, and in prose
   882	$\viableTree(\Sigma)$ abbreviates it.
   883	\end{definition}
   884	
   885	Fork choice uses two derived views (Figure~\ref{alg:fg-store}):
   886	$\Call{get\_fg\_root}{\Sigma}$, the FG root --- the block the walk
   887	starts from --- and
   888	$\Call{get\_filtered\_block\_tree\_from}{\Sigma,\mathit{blocks}}$,
   889	the viable blocks below it in an explicit processed-block view.
   890	$\Call{get\_filtered\_block\_tree}{\Sigma}$ uses the full
   891	$\Sigma.\T$.  Goldfish starts at the root even if the root is not in
   892	the filtered tree.
   893	
   894	At this layer $\Call{goldfish\_eligible}{}$ gains one clause: a child
   895	of a block whose state height is below $\Sigma.\hmax-1$ is eligible
   896	without a majority.  The majority gate thus applies exactly while the walk
   897	stands inside the band $\{\Sigma.\hmax-1,\Sigma.\hmax\}$: every viable
   898	block below the band has a viable child, so the walk always reaches
   899	height $\Sigma.\hmax-1$, and it never passes through the band without a
   900	majority.  Figure~\ref{alg:fg-store} shows the extended store handler, the
   901	extended eligibility condition, and the redefined $\Call{get\_sg\_root}{}$
   902	and $\Call{get\_head}{}$: the SG root is now the deepest block of the
   903	filtered tree holding a relative majority, and the FG root when none does.  Available confirmation is unchanged at
   904	this layer; the complete protocol re-anchors it at the SG root over the
   905	filtered block tree, with the FG root as the fallback value.
   906	
   907	\captionof{figure}{FG store and fork choice}\label{alg:fg-store}
